 The splash reader only reads display MMIO. It retains the existing linear RGB565/8888 formats and contiguous two-half framebuffer checks. Compressed, multirect, overlapping or inconsistent configurations are rejected. A 10-bpc panel specification does not establish the boot framebuffer's format. Real bootloader register state, framebuffer caching/access and actual menu rendering remain untested. This is not a TB323FU installation image or proof of Linux panel support.
 
 Display validation on 2026-10-03 (Docker, build-server): the new dual-scanout fixture compiles against the previous code and fails at the expected success assertion. With the Canoe extension, all 16 ASan/UBSan host tests pass (7.81 seconds), including DMA4/5 and VIG routes, LM6/7, sparse mixer IDs, dummy-active slots, pipe-mask mismatch, unsupported rect/format/compression, out-of-reservation address, duplicate/out-of-range stage offsets, and a changing scanout address. Mock display writes and legacy CTL reads are forbidden by assertions in the Canoe test. The existing HDK8450 capability profile builds a 4 MiB ARM64 runtime with no undefined symbols; all six Manifest QEMU tests pass. These are offline software checks, not TB323FU boot/display acceptance.
+
+Distinct-DTB handoff validation on 2026-10-03
+
+The Manifest QEMU regression now verifies each byte of separate DTBs selected for the base and alternate images. The two fixture blobs are 163 and 167 bytes, so they also expose a packer layout defect: feature/2610 at 2276163 defaults DTB alignment to four bytes, placing the second DTB on an invalid four-mod-eight address. The runtime rejects that Manifest and falls back to the base image.
+
+With the companion packer change (DTB default Align=3, explicit Align<3 rejected), all eight QEMU cases pass using the previously built display-validation runtime. Runtime-DT fallback receives a header check; distinct selected DTBs receive full-byte comparisons. No runtime property merging, real-board memory fixups, initramfs placement or physical boot is tested. The companion packer test also passes its five layout/rejection/round-trip cases. Until that packer change is available, the expanded QEMU test is expected to fail against the unmodified sibling packer.
